  "yoga_title": "Yoga",
  "karmaYoga_title": "Karma",
  "karmaYoga_intro": "Karma Yoga consists of two words. It is a compound word consisting of two words – Karma and Yogaha. The word Karma in this context refers to proper action or appropriate action. The word Yoga means proper attitude or appropriate attitude – in Sanskrit Bhāvanā.",
  "karmaYoga_description": "In simple language Karma Yoga is nothing but proper action with proper attitude. Now we have to understand what is meant by proper action. All the human actions possible for us, are broadly classified into three types by the scriptures. This is based on their spiritual influence on the human beings.\n\nThis is because, the scriptures consider the spiritual personality and the spiritual goal as the most important thing which we cannot ignore and therefore they categorise actions not based on their materialistic benefits and influence. But actions are categorised based on their spiritual influence on you.",
  "karmaYoga_laws": "The four major laws of Karma Yoga are described in Shrimad Bhagvad Gita as follows:\n",
  "karmaYoga_law1": "i. Work with a sense of duty.\n",
  "karmaYoga_law2": "ii. Work without getting intensely attached (focused attention) to the work.\n",
  "karmaYoga_law3": "iii. Never allow the anxieties about the results to interfere with your mind during the currency of the job and\n",
  "karmaYoga_law4": "iv. Accept failure and success with equanimity.\n\n",
  "karma_types_title": "Types of Karma According to Bhagwadgita:\n",
  "karma_types_description": "The way we do actions depends on our personality. In other words all our actions are guided by three tendencies mamely Sattva (सत्त्व), Rajas (रजस्) and Tamas (तमस).\n\nAny action which is undertaken from delusion, without heed to consequence, loss, injury and ability is declared to be Tamasika Karma (तामसिक कर्म).\n\nAction which is done by one craving for desires, or again with egoism or with much effort, is declared to be Rajasika karma (राजसिक कर्म).\n\nHe who is free from all material attachments and false ego, who is enthusiastic and resolute and who is indifferent to success or failure, is a Sattvika karma (सात्विक कर्म) performer of deeds in the righteous path.\n\n",
  "karmaYoga_goal": "The goal of life is to reach highest happiness which is the state of total self- satisfaction. It is a state of pure consciousness, a state from where all actions emerge. This is Nishkamya Karma (निष्काम कर्म) the total non-action/ desireless actions. Though this ultimate goal of life, is total non-action we cannot reach that stated by surrendering action.\n\nThe essence of converting Kamya Karma (काम्य कर्म) into Karma Yoga (कर्मयोग) is in cutting out the strong attachment to fruits of action, which continuously lead us to uncessing work. Asanga (असंग), non-attachment is the key. Unattached work is the process. It is this detachment that counts in Karma Yoga. \nIt is that special attitude, skill that converts \nKamya Karma to Karma Yoga.\n\n",
  "karmaYoga_secrets": "Secrets of Karma Yoga:\n",
  "karmaYoga_secret_description": "It is necessary to discriminate action, forbidden action and inaction. The secrets of Karma Yoga are...\n",
  "karmaYoga_secret": "Perform action.\nBeing fixed in Yoga.\nRenouncing attachments and \nEven minded in success and failure.\n\n",
  "karmaYoga_gita": "Gita doesn't define that Karma is work done to perfection is verily Yoga (Samatva Buddhi, (समत्व बुद्धि). But it advocates that Yoga is dexterity in performing actions. Yogah karmasu kausalam, when you perform an aciton with an equianimous attidtude (Yoga), then such an action does not bind a man into bondage, even though the nature of karma is is to bind.\n",
  "karmaYoga_verse_translation": "He who sees action of Jivatma (जीवात्मा) in inaction and Parmatma's (परमात्मा) inaction in action, he is wise among men, he is a Yogi/Yukta (one who performs actions with equanimous attitude) and accomplisher of everything. In such accomplished men, all impressions of actions are burnt by the fire of knowledge. \nOne who performs actions with this knowledge, attains Jnana and in this fire of knowledge all impressions of actions are burnt\n",
  "bhaktiYoga_title": "Bhakti",
  "bhakti_yoga_overview": "Bhakti Yoga is not a separate path but encompasses all three main spiritual disciplines: Karma Yoga (Path of Action), Upāsana Yoga (Path of Meditation), and Jnāna Yoga (Path of Knowledge). Bhakti Yoga is the overarching attitude of devotion that permeates these practices, making them meaningful and effective.\n\nIn Karma Yoga, Bhakti is expressed by dedicating actions to God and accepting results as divine grace.\n\nIn Upāsana Yoga, Bhakti manifests through meditation and mental worship.\n\nIn Jnāna Yoga, Bhakti reflects in the pursuit of self-realization, equating the self with God.\n\nThus, Bhakti Yoga integrates all forms of discipline into a harmonious practice directed towards spiritual liberation.",
  "types_of_bhakti_title": "The three types of Bhakti, based on the intensity and perspective of love for God, are:\n",
  "manda_bhakti": "Manda Bhakti (Dull or Selfish Bhakti): Love for God as a means to achieve worldly desires or goals. Conditional and dependent on God fulfilling specific wishes. The least intense form of devotion.\n",
  "madhyama_bhakti": "Madhyama Bhakti (Middle-Level Bhakti): Love for God as the ultimate goal of life. God is seen as a representation of peace, happiness, and security. Devotion is more mature and intense than Manda Bhakti.\n",
  "uttama_bhakti": "Uttama Bhakti (Supreme Bhakti): Love for God as non-different from oneself. Recognizes the oneness of the individual self and God. This highest form of devotion equates love for God with self-love.\n",
  "bhakti_definition": "Bhakti is devotion or reverential love directed towards a higher principle, which could be God or anything regarded as sacred, superior, or deserving of respect. It represents love infused with reverence and is an essential concept in spiritual practice. Bhakti can be expressed towards parents (Mātru Bhakti, Pitru Bhakti), a teacher (Guru Bhakti), one’s country (Desha Bhakti), or God (Īshvara Bhakti). The scriptures analyze Bhakti in both its general form (love) and its specific form (love of God).\n",
  "forms_of_bhakti_title": "The nine forms of Bhakti are different ways to express devotion to God, each emphasizing a unique aspect of love:",
  "types_of_bhaktas_title": "Types of Bhaktas described in \nShrimad Bhagavad Gita:\n",
  "types_of_bhaktas_list": "They are -\n· Arta (Distressed one)\n· Jijnasu (Inquisitive one)\n· Artharthee (Desirer of wealth)\n· Jnani (One in search of Absolute knowledge).\n\n",
  "qualities_of_bhakta_title": "Qualities of a Bhakta\n",
  "qualities_of_bhakta_list": "1. Not envious and kind to others\n2. Equipoised\n3. Level-headed\n4. Acts in Ishwarasakshi\n5. Desireless, pure, expert, free from worry\n6. Determined etc.\n\n",
  "dear_to_ishwara": "One who is not envious, but who is a kind friend to all living entities, who does not think himself a proprietor, who is free from false ego and has equal mind both in happiness and in distress, who is always satisfied and engaged in devotional service with determination and whose mind and intelligence are in agreement with Ishwara - he is very dear to Ishwara.\n\nThat devotee of Ishwara who is desireless, pure, expert, free from worry, free from agitation, unconcerned with any mundane endeavour, is very dear to Ishwara. One who grasps neither pleasure nor grief, who neither grieves nor desires, and who renounces both auspicious and inauspicious things, that devotee is very dear to Ishwara.\n\nOne who has equal feeling with his friends as well as enemies, who is equipoised in honour and dishonour, heat and cold, happiness and distress, is very dear to Ishwara.\n\nOne who is silent even when he is praised or reputed, satisfied with whatever comes on its own accord, not attached to domestic life, fixed in determination and engaged in devotional service, is very dear to Ishwara.",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_1_title": "Śravaṇam (Hearing): ",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_1_description": "is hearing about Ishwara. One must always hear about Ishwara when he is reading, recalling or meditating and understand the meaning. As the result of constant Sravanam of Ishwar, the devotee will experience oneness with him.\n",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_2_title": "Kīrtanam (Chanting): ",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_2_description": "is chanting Ishwara's attributes, character, name, power, glory etc. with joy and enthusiasm. It is done either solely or in a group as a public prayer. It increases the devotion to Ishwara and becomes a means of promoting and spreading the love of Ishwara.\n",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_3_title": "Smaranam (Recollecting): ",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_3_description": "is remembering \nthe name, majesty and power of Ishwara.\n",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_4_title": "Pādasevanam (Imbibing): ",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_4_description": "It means walking Guru's (गुरु) (one who has attained Ishwar Sakshatkar) path by imbibing the virtues of Dharma adhered by the Guru in his own life.\n",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_5_title": "Archanam (Worshiping): ",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_5_description": "Worshiping Ishwara with the union of Mind, Word and Deed is called Archana.\n",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_6_title": "Vandanam (Prayer): ",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_6_description": "Invocation and listening to legends/ discourses pertaining to the Ishwara. Vandana is prayer and prostration.\n",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_7_title": "Dāsyam (Service): ",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_7_description": "is the Service of Ishwara. In spite of the identity between the soul and Ishwara, the devotee should always consider that he is one of the humblest servants of Ishwara, which enhance the cause of Ishwara-realization.\n",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_8_title": "Sakhyam (Friendship): ",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_8_description": "In Sakhya-Bhava devotee treats Ishwara as a friend. To be always with the Ishwara, to treat Him as one's own dear friend, to be in His company at all times, to love Him as one's own Self, is Sakhya-Bhava of Bhakti-Marga.\n",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_9_title": "Ātmanivedanam (Offering): ",
  "bhakti_nine_forms_9_description": "The devotee offers everything to Ishwara, including his body, mind and soul. He keeps nothing for himself. He loses even his own self. He has no personal and independent existence. He totally offers himself to Ishwara, which is called Atma Nivaedana.\n",
  "jnana_title": "Jnana",
  "jnanayoga_intro": "Jnanayoga is a stream of yoga that involves Buddhi (बुद्धि) (intellect), self- introspection and chintan (चिन्तन) that leads to the ultimate realization that Jiva (जीव) and Brahma are inseparable (Moksha).\n\nTo progress in Jnanayoga, one pointedness, discipline and a sharp intellect is required, because only Jnana leads to Saksahtkar (to know things as they truly are).",
  "jnanayoga_what_is_jnana": "What is Jnana?",
  "jnanayoga_sbg_reference": "In Shrimadbhagvadgita Jnanayoga is referred to as Samkhyayoga, which states that Mukti (मुक्ति) is possible with Tattvajnana (तत्वज्ञान); but in practice the sadhana of Vedanta is also known as Jnanayoga.\n\nAccording to the theory of Jnanayoga, the Atman (Paramatma) is Aananda-svaroopa (आनन्द स्वरूप), Jnanasvaroopa (ज्ञानस्वरूप), Satya (सत्य), Nitya (नित्य) and Shuddha (शुद्ध). Nothing other than Brahman exists (permanent) in this world. This entire world is illuminated and situated in Brahman. Brahman itself is the illuminator and although its fire is one, it manifests in various forms.",
  "jnanayoga_classification_intro": "Jnanayoga sadhana is classified into two:\n",
  "jnanayoga_bahiranga_intro": "Bahiranga sadhana - ",
  "jnanayoga_bahiranga_1": "for beginners.\n",
  "jnanayoga_antaranga_intro": "Antaranga sadhana - ",
  "jnanayoga_antaranga_1":"for advanced seekers.\n\n",
  "jnanayoga_bahiranga_sadhana": "Bahiranga sadhana (बहिरंग साधन): - \n",
  "four_pillars":"The four pillars are: - \n\n",
  "jnanayoga_bahiranga_pillars": "· Viveka (विवेक)\n· Vairagya (वैराग्य)\n· Shata Sampati (षट् सम्पत्ति)\n· Mumukshatva (मुमुक्षुत्व)\n\n",
  "jnanayoga_bahiranga_details": "Refer Homepage for detailed information.\nThe practitioner, who adopts and practices these four pillars of Bahiranga sadhana of Jnanayoga, deserves to achieve Mukti (मुक्ति) (Salvation) - freedom from Kama (काम) (Lust), Krodh (क्रोध) (Anger), Lobh (लोभ) (Greed), Moha (मोह) (Attachment), Mada (Arrogance) and Matsarya (मात्सर्य) (Jealousy), which is the root cause of dukhas (दुःख).",
  "jnanayoga_antaranga_sadhana": "Antaranga sadhana (अंतरंग साधन): - ",
  "jnanayoga_shravana": "· Shravana (श्रवण)\n",
  "jnanayoga_shravana_description":"To listen and comprehend knowledge (subtlest wisdom) about Brahma, preachings of scholars, yogis and Guru about Brahma with utmost attention and peaceful mind is Shravana.\n",
  "jnanayoga_manana": "· Manana (मनन)\n",
  "jnanayoga_manana_description":"Process of repeatedly recollecting the knowledge and wisdom that one hears, and eliminating any doubts that arise by questioning the speaker. Just as an animal masticates its food, a sincere sadhaka or aspirant recollects and masticates on the teachings that he/she has partaken.\n",
  "jnanayoga_nididhyasana": "· Nididhyasana (निदिध्यासन)\n",
  "jnanayoga_nididhyasana_description":"After Shravana and Manana, when the sadhaka establishes the heard knowledge in hrdaya i.e., adopts it in his/her Acharana (conduct) and abides in the teachings, it is Nididhyasana.\n\n",
  "jnanayoga_nididhyasana_components": "According to Aparokshanubhuti, Nididhyasana has fifteen components: Yama (यम), Niyama (नियम), Tyaga (त्याग), Mauna (मौन), Desha (देश), Kaala (काल), Asana (आसन), Moolabandha (मूलबंध), Deha (भक्ति), Sthiti (सृष्टी), Pranayama (प्रत्याहार), Pratyahara, Dharana (धारणा), Dhyana (ध्यान) and Atma Sakshatkar (आत्म साक्षात्कार) with Atma Sakshatkar being the ultimate state of Sadhana.\n",
  "jnanayoga_atma_sakshatkar": "· Atma Sakshatkar (आत्म साक्षात्कार)\n",
  "jnanayoga_atma_sakshatkar_description":"When the sadhaka reaches the highest state in his/her Sadhana, and recognizes his/her true svaroop and is able to synchronize himself/herself with the Paramatattva (परमतत्व), he/she becomes eligible for Atma-sakshatkar.\n\n",
  "jnanayoga_jnani_qualities": "Qualities of a Jnani (ज्ञानी)\n",
  "jnanayoga_sbg_2_18_19": "When a man abandons all the desires of the heart and is satisfied in the Self alone by the Self, then he is said to be one of steady wisdom. One whose mind remains undisturbed amidst misery, who does not crave for pleasure, and who is free from attachment, fear, and anger, is called a sage of steady wisdom.\n\nAccording to (S.B.G-2.18-19): The man of Shraddha (श्रद्धा), the devoted and the master of his senses obtains knowledge. Having obtained this knowledge, he attains supreme peace. This is the Brahmi (ब्रह्ममी) state. Attaining this, none is bewildered. Being established in it even at the death-hour, a man gets into oneness with Brahman (ब्रह्म).\n",
  "jnanayoga_seek_enlightenment": "When you seek that enlightenment by prostrating, questioning and performing selfless service; \nthe wise, the seers into the Truth \nwill instruct you in that knowledge.",
  "what_is_yoga": "What is Yoga ?",
  "what_is_yoga_desc": "Keeping all the goals and especially the spiritual goal of Moksha in mind the scriptures prescribe a course of discipline which we generally call Sādhanā. Sādhanā means a course of discipline that we undertake to accomplish a goal – the goal is called Sādhyam.\n\nTo accomplish the Sādhyam, the goal, we take to a course of discipline called Sādhanā and when we take to this discipline we are called Sādhaka. Therefore the scriptures want us to become Sādhakas taking to Sādhanā for accomplishing the Sādhyam.\n\nAnd once we accomplish the Sādhyam, we will be become Siddhāhā. So, Sādhaka through Sādhanā should attain Sādhyam and thus become Siddha. Sādhaka to Siddha is the journey of life. Siddha is not related to miraculous powers. Siddha is one who is totally at home with himself. \n\nAnd this entire course of Sādhanā is broadly classified or divided into three levels or segments for the convenience of understanding and practice. Each level being called a Yogaha.\n\nThe very word Yoga meant that which links the Sādhaka and the Sādhyam – the seeker and the sought. Yoga is derived from the root Yuj – to combine or unite. Unite the Sādhaka and Sādhyam.\n",
